1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Gadgets. For the most part they come and go.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
But a few stick around to become necessities.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
They are the sharp and the sharper.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
They keep the darkness at bay and defy the wind.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
They keep the beat rock steady.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
They fit comfortably into the palm of your hand.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And they're just getting started.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, more gadgets on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:50,000
Gadgets. And more gadgets.

10
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:55,400
Don't be scared of them. You can always, so far, turn them off.

11
00:00:55,401 --> 00:00:58,560
The changes we're seeing are extraordinary.

12
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,860
They are happening faster every day.

13
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:02,780
Don't blink.

14
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:08,380
The good news is, at least for the moment, we're in control.

15
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:10,440
Control.

16
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,520
That is, to a large extent, what we desire from our gadgets.

17
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:21,600
Only a tiny fraction of these novelties will evolve into valued necessities.

18
00:01:21,601 --> 00:01:26,000
But that doesn't stop today's global high-gloss gadget trade.

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,600
Billion-dollar boutiqueries have sprung up on the lowly backs of doohickeys and whatchamacallits.

20
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:42,980
But when it comes to gadgets, corporate giants and market research have yet to replace the individual entrepreneur with a heart and a hunch.

21
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,860
Gadget is a passion.

22
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:58,880
Every person comes with his own product, with that product that can change his lifestyle, that can make him feel, you know, different or do things easier.

23
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:13,174
Alex El Nacave, engineer and inventor, is the CEO of Gadget Universe, the mail order catalog business he founded in 1997, and that currently generates gadget sales of

24
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:14,700
$40 million a year.

25
00:02:14,701 --> 00:02:15,980
I like the color.

26
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:16,780
Impressive.

27
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:19,320
But Gadget Universe is the new kid on the block.

28
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,220
The David to sharper images Goliath.

29
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:25,180
The Gadget Game is fiercely competitive,

30
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,560
so you've got to expect a little bravado from the combatants.

31
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:30,780
What competition?

32
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,740
We don't have competition.

33
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,560
That's why we called ourselves the leader of innovations.

34
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:40,520
Gadget Universe buyers have a knack for the nifty.

35
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:44,260
They search the world for the grand prize of the Gadget Game.

36
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:47,100
an elusive, original idea.

37
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:52,580
Gadget is an item that you've never seen before.

38
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,460
I think that's where it starts.

39
00:02:54,920 --> 00:03:00,260
I mean, 10,000 years ago, probably the wheels was the biggest gadget ever.

40
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:04,480
Each month at highly charged product meetings,

41
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,700
Alex and his specialists sift through hundreds of gadget wannabes

42
00:03:08,701 --> 00:03:11,600
and decide what's in and what's out.

43
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:13,980
Okay, look, these are called light cubes.

44
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,900
They're silly. I know that. I realize that. That's part of the charm.

45
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,280
What are you going to do? You tap them on. Boom. Right there. They go in your drink.

46
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,520
Ultimately, gut instinct.

47
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,680
And anyone's informed guests are the only guides to gadget glory.

48
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,520
The goal is to be the first with the unknown.

49
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:34,940
Without a doubt.

50
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:38,380
It's very nice. It sounds very, very cute.

51
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:40,580
Carry on. I'll veto that.

52
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:42,400
This is called the Soulmate.

53
00:03:42,401 --> 00:03:47,100
This is an MP3 device. It goes into this unit called the Music Store.

54
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:50,600
We're talking 200 CDs worth of music here.

55
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:55,420
So you can take your whole collection, download it into here, call it up at will,

56
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,460
and your whole collection is here digitally ready to roll.

57
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:04,100
Gadget Universe needs 20 to 30 hit gadgets a month to stay in the game.

58
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:08,440
It's a wrist camera watch. It holds 100 black and white pictures.

59
00:04:08,441 --> 00:04:13,220
Simply take it, click it, take a picture, and you can download that straight to your PC.

60
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:17,900
It seems there are three big questions that have to be answered about every product.

61
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:19,300
Is it useful?

62
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:21,120
What do you mean you can put it on the PC?

63
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:22,580
You can download the picture to your PC?

64
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:23,520
Download it.

65
00:04:23,521 --> 00:04:25,160
And then can you edit it?

66
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:25,920
Yes.

67
00:04:25,921 --> 00:04:26,621
Then print it out?

68
00:04:26,622 --> 00:04:27,322
That's great.

69
00:04:27,323 --> 00:04:28,023
That's a hot item.

70
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:29,200
Will it sell?

71
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:33,220
I think that fits beautifully with the demographic we're looking for.

72
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:36,320
And will it look cool in the catalog?

73
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:37,660
What do you guys think?

74
00:04:37,661 --> 00:04:38,680
I say go with it.

75
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:39,400
Come on.

76
00:04:39,401 --> 00:04:40,101
That's two-two.

77
00:04:40,102 --> 00:04:41,220
Two-two.

78
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:44,740
For Gadget Universe, the catalog is everything.

79
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:47,640
Two million copies are mailed each month,

80
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:52,340
and on its glossy pages, the big selling items quickly crowd out the flops.

81
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:57,340
Other gadgets are considered image items.

82
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:02,240
Even if they're not terribly useful, they make the catalog more impressive.

83
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:07,360
The practical benefits of this infrared thermometer may be questioned,

84
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,140
but its coolness is beyond reproach.

85
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:17,100
Ultimately, though, the lifespan of a gadget is decided by what it can do to enrich a person's life.

86
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:26,000
The first great gadget of the 21st century is the Personal Digital Assistant, or PDA.

87
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:34,980
The Palm Handheld Computer and its rivals, such as the Compaq iPack and the Handspring Visor,

88
00:05:34,981 --> 00:05:39,740
are the gotta-have gadget for what may become a post-paper generation.

89
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Handheld computers generated over a billion dollars in sales in 2000.

90
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:51,680
Once a gadget, now a way of life.

91
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:56,200
Really what Palm is about is making the ultimate piece of paper.

92
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:03,500
Forerunner of the PDA craze, the original Palm Pilot was introduced in 1995.

93
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:08,980
coming on the heels of Apple's Newton, which was largely rejected by consumers.

94
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:12,760
The palm was positioned as a simple electronic organizer.

95
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:16,740
But it proved to be much more.

96
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:19,720
It did something the world had never seen before.

97
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:23,360
Put computing power in the palm of your hand.

98
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:27,100
It's just like we took all of that stuff and we just made it really, really tiny.

99
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:29,800
But we also did a lot of work on the user interface, too.

100
00:06:29,801 --> 00:06:35,460
And we also did a lot of work on figuring out what is it that you really want to do with this thing.

101
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:39,760
We weren't sure that in fact it was going to be very successful.

102
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:43,840
And the success of the products just absolutely amazed us.

103
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:46,400
It was so easy to use.

104
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:51,960
All it did was organize your personal data, your address book or your date book.

105
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:53,760
But it became so powerful.

106
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:56,840
I think that for me, the palm is my world.

107
00:06:56,841 --> 00:07:02,240
and the reason I say that is because I keep absolutely everything in my life in my palm

108
00:07:02,241 --> 00:07:04,480
and I can no longer live without it.

109
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:07,320
Deceptively simple on the outside,

110
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,960
50 years of technology are tucked inside.

111
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:14,460
The basic components are the case,

112
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:16,780
a pen-like stylus,

113
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:18,880
a 4-inch screen,

114
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:23,260
and a board of integrated circuits which stores the basic programs.

115
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:26,020
This is what's inside. This is the gut.

116
00:07:26,021 --> 00:07:30,080
So this is the heart and soul, if you will, of these products.

117
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:35,180
The original Pilot and the original, in this case, now the Palm 5, our more recent product.

118
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,760
Software links the handheld with a desk or laptop computer.

119
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:47,740
The first generation of the Palm was soon eclipsed by more powerful descendants.

120
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:52,400
mirroring the internet explosion

121
00:07:52,401 --> 00:07:56,220
the palm five can surf the web with astonishing ease

122
00:07:56,221 --> 00:08:00,500
expandability is the new buzzword

123
00:08:00,501 --> 00:08:04,300
making it possible to piggyback gadgets onto gadgets

124
00:08:04,301 --> 00:08:08,420
i'm finding that i'm running out of pockets

125
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:10,140
the solution

126
00:08:10,141 --> 00:08:13,800
pdas are rapidly converging into the gadget

127
00:08:13,801 --> 00:08:15,940
to end all gadgets

128
00:08:15,941 --> 00:08:20,700
The latest palm can become your TV remote control,

129
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:24,320
compass,

130
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:27,760
chess opponent,

131
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:34,560
and even a mirror into your gadget-loving soul.

132
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:42,300
It was only a matter of time before the PDA merged with that other highly coveted device,

133
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:43,700
the cell phone.

134
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,140
The possibilities are limitless.

135
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:55,000
In the future, this clear case represents how our products will hold a variety of expansion options.

136
00:08:55,380 --> 00:09:00,300
So about the size of a postage stamp in the future, we'll be able to insert, in this case, a camera.

137
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:05,040
The handheld computer may be the latest in high-tech gadgetry,

138
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:08,740
but the real cutting edge was born over 100 years ago.

139
00:09:09,260 --> 00:09:11,740
Next, the story of the safety razor.

140
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:21,600
On the first day that Palm stock was offered to the public, the price of one share rose from $38 to a high of $165.

141
00:09:23,380 --> 00:09:25,740
More gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

142
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:37,420
It's hard to imagine that only 100 years ago, a man had little control over his own beard.

143
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:41,880
If the familiar ritual of shaving sometimes seems tedious,

144
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:46,740
in earlier forms, it was downright brutal.

145
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,960
An ingenious gadget at the beginning of the 20th century would change all that.

146
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:58,640
But the story of razors begins with whiskers of a much earlier era.

147
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,140
Well, shaving actually starts out in the caveman days.

148
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:05,380
This is a North American flint knife,

149
00:10:05,381 --> 00:10:10,520
but it really typifies the kind of shaving device that early man used.

150
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:13,340
Now he really wasn't trying to get a close shave

151
00:10:13,341 --> 00:10:17,300
but what he was trying to do is minimize the breeding grounds for vermin

152
00:10:17,301 --> 00:10:21,540
maybe eat a little easier and also minimize a handhold in battle.

153
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:27,540
By the 1400s, advances in metallurgy had made possible

154
00:10:27,541 --> 00:10:30,600
a shaving tool that was only slightly more evolved.

155
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:35,280
The straight razor was a cruel, bare blade with a handle.

156
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:41,340
For centuries, it was forged out of sheer steel by blacksmiths, one at a time.

157
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:47,300
Adding insult to possible injury, it was too expensive for the common man.

158
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:53,840
The barber wielded the hair-raising power of the blade, and the public shave became a way of life.

159
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:59,520
Only lather relieved the assault of steel on skin.

160
00:10:59,521 --> 00:11:05,360
The barbers of that day brought the lather in a lather bowl such as this

161
00:11:05,361 --> 00:11:09,960
and brought it over to the patron where they used a brush to brush onto the person's face.

162
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:20,500
With small changes in style, the gruesome straight razor was the shaving technology for 600 years of nicks and gouges.

163
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:25,660
In the 19th century, manufacturers began promoting self-shaving

164
00:11:25,661 --> 00:11:29,260
with fancy straight razors that only the rich could afford.

165
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,560
Some even had handles of pearl and brass.

166
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:37,780
But nothing could disguise the serious design flaw of the straight razor.

167
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,840
With a slip of a hand, it could slit your throat.

168
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:49,420
In 1880, it was the Camphy brothers, Richard, Frederick and Otto,

169
00:11:49,780 --> 00:11:52,780
who finally got serious about the bloody business of shaving.

170
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:57,560
A simple innovation took the edge off the ordeal.

171
00:11:57,561 --> 00:12:04,380
This is the first real safety razor, the Campfe Brothers Star.

172
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:11,220
And what this was, was a section of a straight razor that was cut out and put into a holder,

173
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:17,200
which had a comb guard and allowed the user to shave without cutting their face.

174
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:23,440
On the Campfe Brothers Star razor, as little as 6 millimeters of the actual blade was exposed,

175
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:26,200
roughly the same as today's razors.

176
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:31,480
The blades were sharpened on a leather strap while still in the blade holder.

177
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:36,680
The star razor was only the first step in making shaving easy for the common man.

178
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:44,480
But soon, a young dreamer, an anti-war socialist who longed to come up with something useful for the working man,

179
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,700
would shake the shaving establishment.

180
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:54,560
In 1885, 30-year-old King Camp Gillette was a salesman for William Painter,

181
00:12:54,561 --> 00:12:56,980
the man who invented the disposable bottle cap.

182
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:00,960
William Painter said,

183
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:05,920
my advice to you is to invent something that can be only used once,

184
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:08,800
like my bottle cap, and then throw it away.

185
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:12,820
And the customers will have to keep coming back to you over and over again.

186
00:13:13,660 --> 00:13:17,760
And so King Gillette decided that he was going to come up with a disposable item,

187
00:13:17,761 --> 00:13:19,000
but he didn't know what it was.

188
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:23,100
And he thought about that for three or four years.

189
00:13:23,101 --> 00:13:28,040
Gillette decided to make a razor that even a poor man could afford.

190
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:31,160
A safety razor with a throwaway blade.

191
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:37,320
And he determined that you really didn't need a thick blade with a sharp edge.

192
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:39,220
All you needed was a sharp edge.

193
00:13:39,980 --> 00:13:43,320
And to save money, you would have a very thin blade.

194
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:50,220
After tinkering for five years, Gillette introduced the world's first disposable blade.

195
00:13:50,221 --> 00:13:55,340
And so this is one of the very earliest razors that King Gillette ever made.

196
00:13:55,500 --> 00:14:01,200
It was probably produced in 1903 or 1904, even before he received his patent.

197
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:05,100
And this took a blade, something like this.

198
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:10,100
It was very thin, six thousandths of an inch thick, three holes,

199
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:14,060
so it could be positioned in the razor with the positioning studs.

200
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:20,080
The blade sold in packs of 12, with the razor handle a one-time purchase.

201
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:31,603
If a man could just purchase his own razor, he could do a fast job of shaving, could get to the office in time, have more time to spend with his family and to do other

202
00:14:31,604 --> 00:14:32,304
things.

203
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:35,360
This was quite an improvement in a man's life.

204
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:40,820
Gillette sold only 168 blades his first year in business.

205
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:48,560
People were saying, King is asking us to use this blade until it's dull and then just throw it away and get another one.

206
00:14:48,561 --> 00:14:51,960
and we really aren't comfortable with that idea.

207
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:56,220
But clever advertising campaigns helped lure a skeptical public

208
00:14:56,221 --> 00:14:58,700
away from their 19th century sensibilities.

209
00:15:01,740 --> 00:15:05,200
It started off the approach, years and years later,

210
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:06,760
of disposing of a lot of things.

211
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:11,680
One year later, he sold 2.4 million blades.

212
00:15:13,080 --> 00:15:16,000
Sometimes the simplest of gadgets, like the safety razor,

213
00:15:16,001 --> 00:15:20,780
provides value far beyond what anybody would expect.

214
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:26,420
And by 1910, King Camp Gillette was an uneasy millionaire,

215
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:30,880
a socialist railing against capitalism and the evils of war.

216
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:35,380
Ironically, it was the Great War that secured his legacy.

217
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:41,640
This is a khaki set that was put out by Gillette in World War I.

218
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:45,280
And in fact, Gillette won the contract with the U.S. government

219
00:15:45,281 --> 00:15:49,580
So they provided all the safety razors to all the soldiers that went away.

220
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:56,240
What happened after World War I is that the servicemen were allowed to take their razors home with them,

221
00:15:56,440 --> 00:15:59,160
and so that was a ready market of three million shavers.

222
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:02,880
Gillette became an international folk hero.

223
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:08,080
His name was attached to his product, and his photograph was on every blade wrapper.

224
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:11,900
King Cap Gillette died in 1932.

225
00:16:11,901 --> 00:16:16,360
His legacy? Changing the face of the world.

226
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:18,400
We're very proud.

227
00:16:20,140 --> 00:16:21,920
But it was a shared legacy.

228
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:30,140
In 1926, Colonel Jacob Schick, a veteran of World War I, invented the magazine repeating razor.

229
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,880
He took his Army experiences with a bolt-action rifle

230
00:16:34,881 --> 00:16:38,940
and determined that the shaving head could be put on its side

231
00:16:38,941 --> 00:16:42,760
and a number of blades could be put in the handle

232
00:16:42,761 --> 00:16:46,340
so you could actually inject a new blade

233
00:16:46,341 --> 00:16:48,840
as you would injecting a bullet in a rifle

234
00:16:48,841 --> 00:16:52,420
and then turn this back around like this for shaving purposes.

235
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:55,460
One great gadget begets another.

236
00:16:56,800 --> 00:17:00,120
In 1929, Jacob Schick introduced his pet project,

237
00:17:00,121 --> 00:17:02,360
which he called the Dry Shaver.

238
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:05,700
It was the world's first electric razor.

239
00:17:05,701 --> 00:17:12,760
Over the next 50 years, the electric shaver would evolve into a sleek, highly coveted gadget.

240
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:20,340
Innovations in form and function blossomed, led by European manufacturers Norelco and Braun.

241
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:30,740
Today's cutting-edge electrics sound like sports cars, boasting LED oil indicators and promising to deliver 13,000 strokes per minute.

242
00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:34,640
Some release a dash of skin conditioner.

243
00:17:35,700 --> 00:17:37,700
others are self-cleaning.

244
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:42,700
Ironically, the dry shaver is a wet shaver again,

245
00:17:42,701 --> 00:17:46,700
as most of these cordless wonders work with water and foam.

246
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:53,700
However, more than three out of four men still prefer the silent stroke of the safety razor.

247
00:17:55,700 --> 00:18:00,700
Today's safety razors are manufactured using precision technology

248
00:18:00,701 --> 00:18:08,100
and an army of brave test subjects who try out the blades one beard at a time.

249
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:16,100
Multiple blades spaced exactly 60 thousandths of an inch apart dominate the market.

250
00:18:19,520 --> 00:18:23,560
Schick and Gillette are butting heads with dueling triple blade safety razors.

251
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:28,160
Gillette has spent close to a billion dollars marketing its Mach 3.

252
00:18:28,161 --> 00:18:35,160
Not a lot when you consider that its Boston factory alone produces 5 billion razor blades per year.

253
00:18:39,160 --> 00:18:42,160
In gadget history, the razor was certainly a sharp idea.

254
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:46,160
The flashlight, on the other hand, was a bright one.

255
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,160
Up next, gadgets that glow.

256
00:18:49,161 --> 00:18:50,001
that glow.

257
00:18:50,002 --> 00:18:56,480
In a 1977 interview, Fidel Castro revealed that he never would have grown his

258
00:18:56,481 --> 00:19:01,520
trademark fear had his supply of Gillette razor blades not been cut off during his guerrilla

259
00:19:01,521 --> 00:19:04,179
days in the mountains of Cuba.

260
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:09,320
More gadgets will return on Modern Marvel's powerful iNova

261
00:19:09,321 --> 00:19:14,760
microlight. It boasts one mile visibility and a 100,000 hour bulb.

262
00:19:14,761 --> 00:19:16,820
We look at the flashlight

263
00:19:16,821 --> 00:19:19,880
is sort of the, almost like the most popular kid in the class.

264
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,220
Nobody dislikes a flashlight.

265
00:19:23,180 --> 00:19:26,900
The flashlight, a novelty that evolved into a necessity.

266
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:32,040
So much so that you can find three of them in the average American home.

267
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:37,640
Fittingly, its birth is rooted in entrepreneurial myth.

268
00:19:40,140 --> 00:19:43,420
Lionel Cowan, the owner of the American Electric Novelty Company,

269
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:45,760
imagined a lighted flower pot.

270
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:53,560
He passed on the idea to his traveling salesman, a down-on-his-luck Russian immigrant named Conrad Hubert.

271
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:57,860
He was in the farming business and was a failure at that.

272
00:19:58,580 --> 00:20:02,600
He delivered milk on a horse and wagon and was a failure at that,

273
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:07,000
but was always fascinated with electricity and novelties.

274
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:13,260
As he tinkered with a metal tube, battery, and bulb, an unexpected idea blossomed.

275
00:20:16,820 --> 00:20:21,820
When Hubert put the parts together, he had invented the world's first flashlight.

276
00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:30,820
Conrad Hubert went door to door, persuading a skeptical public that the electric torch was portable sunshine.

277
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:40,820
He gave the new gadget to policemen, who soon found portable electric light indispensable on the dark, dangerous night watch.

278
00:20:40,821 --> 00:20:48,540
Only one year later, the gadget was well on its way to becoming a fixture

279
00:20:48,541 --> 00:20:51,620
Conrad Hubert was a millionaire

280
00:20:51,621 --> 00:20:55,120
And the president of the Ever Ready Battery Company

281
00:20:55,121 --> 00:20:58,200
It's the American dream, is what it is

282
00:20:58,201 --> 00:21:02,280
The flashlight has always been the great American gadget

283
00:21:02,281 --> 00:21:06,840
Early advertising managed to turn a cold metal tube

284
00:21:06,841 --> 00:21:10,240
Into a warm metaphor for the American way of life

285
00:21:10,241 --> 00:21:17,240
But behind the glowing image, the focus was the basics of bulbs and batteries.

286
00:21:17,241 --> 00:21:23,240
First of all, you need a case, and you also need batteries.

287
00:21:23,241 --> 00:21:27,240
You need a switch to turn this light on and off.

288
00:21:27,241 --> 00:21:30,240
You need a contact strip.

289
00:21:30,241 --> 00:21:34,240
You need a reflector, and you also need a bulb.

290
00:21:34,241 --> 00:21:38,240
The flashlight's design was brilliantly simple.

291
00:21:38,241 --> 00:21:41,860
But the battery of 1898 was flawed.

292
00:21:42,820 --> 00:21:47,660
It was six inches tall, weighed three pounds, and didn't last very long.

293
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:53,840
Battery technology was very, very basic and very, very crude.

294
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:58,980
A battery would last about six hours, maybe, tops, because they would leak.

295
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:05,640
The very first switches flipped on to a metal contact point to activate the light.

296
00:22:05,641 --> 00:22:13,820
To conserve the undependable battery, people had to navigate through the night by flashing the light on and off.

297
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:16,640
Hence the name, flashlight.

298
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:22,640
A better flashlight required a longer-lasting battery.

299
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:26,820
Batteries are a self-contained electrochemical system.

300
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:34,040
Every battery has a positive and negative electrode, separated by some form of electrolyte.

301
00:22:34,041 --> 00:22:39,420
a non-metallic conductor that permits the passage of charged particles called ions.

302
00:22:40,980 --> 00:22:45,420
The highest power density is found in batteries with an alkaline electrolyte.

303
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:55,980
Alkaline batteries were first introduced in the 1880s and are still flourishing today.

304
00:22:57,720 --> 00:23:03,580
With more concentrated power, batteries got smaller and bulbs got brighter.

305
00:23:04,040 --> 00:23:09,080
From the beginning, battery shape has followed the form of a flashlight,

306
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:12,680
and the flashlight reflected the fashion of the day.

307
00:23:14,520 --> 00:23:20,160
Sterling silver, and the bulb is here, and the battery is right inside.

308
00:23:20,840 --> 00:23:22,840
Here we have an Art Deco light.

309
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:27,060
If you're going to get up in the middle of the night or you wanted to see what time it was,

310
00:23:27,600 --> 00:23:31,760
you just lift the bulb, and voila, there was light.

311
00:23:31,761 --> 00:23:35,340
You could also use it as a flashlight or a spotlight.

312
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:38,380
And the lantern light?

313
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:43,020
It resembles the one kicked over by Mrs. O'Leary's Chicago cow,

314
00:23:43,460 --> 00:23:46,340
a sly reminder of the hazards of not owning a flashlight.

315
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,960
We like to cast the flashlight into the role of the hero.

316
00:23:52,340 --> 00:23:55,680
A flashlight is a tool. A flashlight is a lifesaver.

317
00:23:55,860 --> 00:23:56,660
It's an enabler.

318
00:23:56,661 --> 00:24:01,560
It allows people to enjoy a lot of the things that we take for granted.

319
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:08,100
The flashlight has come full circle, from novelty to necessity,

320
00:24:08,640 --> 00:24:12,980
and is now once again on the forefront of gadgetry.

321
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:16,840
But there's more than one gadget to answer the question.

322
00:24:17,820 --> 00:24:18,520
Got a light?

323
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:21,520
Hey, that beautiful girl wants a light.

324
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:24,280
Well, this is more like it.

325
00:24:24,281 --> 00:24:28,160
That man has a zippo, the lighter that always lights with one zip.

326
00:24:28,161 --> 00:24:31,960
Bradford, Pennsylvania, 1931.

327
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:36,580
From the darkness of the Depression came the spark of an idea

328
00:24:36,581 --> 00:24:40,580
that would lead to a gadget of the First Order.

329
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:45,480
George Blaisdell, an oil man in financial trouble,

330
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:47,520
gambled his last $800,

331
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:52,820
buying the dubious design rights to a clunky-looking Austrian lighter

332
00:24:52,821 --> 00:24:54,720
that claimed to be windproof.

333
00:24:54,721 --> 00:25:03,900
Blaisdell rented a cramped shop, made a few lighters, sold none, and then began to tinker.

334
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:07,820
Until he crafted something that worked.

335
00:25:09,720 --> 00:25:13,700
The secret was the hood that protected the flame from wind and rain.

336
00:25:14,760 --> 00:25:17,280
Its classic form balanced its classic function.

337
00:25:18,580 --> 00:25:19,460
Portable fire.

338
00:25:21,020 --> 00:25:23,660
Blaisdell's tinkering produced an American original.

339
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:27,880
But a preoccupied public had yet to discover it.

340
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:36,880
In 1933, during his first month of business, he sold just 82 Zippos at $1.95 each.

341
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:40,040
Just another gadget.

342
00:25:42,340 --> 00:25:44,540
But Blaisdell had a hunch.

343
00:25:45,300 --> 00:25:51,840
When an early customer returned a lighter for repairs, Blaisdell improvised the famous Zippo Lifetime Guarantee.

344
00:25:51,841 --> 00:25:56,540
and word of the lighter's reliability spread.

345
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:01,820
But it was World War II that kept the Zippo from flaming out,

346
00:26:02,900 --> 00:26:06,400
turning a gadget on the edge into a military necessity.

347
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:12,200
During the war, every Zippo manufactured was purchased by the U.S. government

348
00:26:12,201 --> 00:26:14,600
and distributed to American troops.

349
00:26:18,740 --> 00:26:20,940
Soldiers used the Zippo on the battlefield.

350
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:26,280
For everything from lighting signal fires to tapping out secret codes.

351
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:30,260
More than one GI has sworn that it stopped a bullet.

352
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:37,080
War correspondent Ernie Pyle wrote to Blaisdell to thank him on behalf of the enlisted man.

353
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:43,020
General Douglas MacArthur used the Zippo to light his pipe.

354
00:26:44,760 --> 00:26:48,040
Dwight Eisenhower called it, the lighter we can light any time.

355
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:51,000
But it was something more.

356
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:58,200
As the legend of the lighter grew, it became an enduring emblem of artful Americana around the world.

357
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:06,480
Today, Zippo produces 16 million lighters a year and is closing in on three...

358
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,540
Still, that's only a fraction of the lighter market.

359
00:27:13,541 --> 00:27:19,220
The disposable Bic, made famous by the ubiquitous Flick My Bic campaign,

360
00:27:20,020 --> 00:27:23,320
sells 4 million lighters worldwide every day.

361
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:30,680
But the Zippo stands alone as a coveted gadget, and to some, a hero.

362
00:27:32,020 --> 00:27:35,380
Appropriately, it's joining forces with that other light-bearing hero

363
00:27:35,381 --> 00:27:39,180
to create a hybrid gadget called the Ziplight.

364
00:27:39,181 --> 00:27:44,180
People have always loved gadgets that play music.

365
00:27:44,181 --> 00:27:49,500
Up next, astounding devices that blur the line between musician and instrument.

366
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:55,180
We now return to more gadgets on Modern Marvels.

367
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:02,720
The drum machine drives today's music with sophisticated digital beats.

368
00:28:02,721 --> 00:28:09,440
but its lineage can be traced to the first musical gadget that could keep a beat

369
00:28:09,441 --> 00:28:16,600
the metronome it clicks at an adjustable pace to mimic tempo

370
00:28:16,601 --> 00:28:23,560
it's designed to keep perfect time for you what so you can practice your scales

371
00:28:23,561 --> 00:28:28,640
it's really a tool a device to to keep you in time when you practice any instrument

372
00:28:28,641 --> 00:28:35,940
The original is as familiar as a piano lesson, but making a click took centuries.

373
00:28:37,460 --> 00:28:41,020
Galileo was the first to note the consistency of the pendulum swing

374
00:28:41,021 --> 00:28:46,840
when he compared the movement of a swinging lamp in a Pisa cathedral with his own pulse rate.

375
00:28:48,040 --> 00:28:52,700
But making an adjustable pendulum proved elusive up until 1812

376
00:28:52,701 --> 00:28:58,580
when a Dutchman, Dietrich Nikolaus Winkle, solved the problem by designing a pendulum.

377
00:28:58,640 --> 00:29:00,380
with a double-weighted pivot.

378
00:29:02,520 --> 00:29:07,100
Four years later, a well-connected musical tinkerer named Johann Malzell

379
00:29:07,101 --> 00:29:11,340
seized on the idea and manufactured Malzell's metronome.

380
00:29:11,780 --> 00:29:16,260
He passed the new invention on to his friend, Ludwig von Beethoven,

381
00:29:16,860 --> 00:29:19,380
who became the first prominent user of the metronome.

382
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:23,180
He even wrote a brief musical tribute to his new gadget.

383
00:29:23,181 --> 00:29:32,220
In his eighth symphony, the second movement's ticking, pulsing beat was meant to mimic the precise click of the metronome.

384
00:29:33,420 --> 00:29:37,180
To this day, the Malzell sets the standard for the mechanical metronome.

385
00:29:41,040 --> 00:29:50,400
It's housed in a pyramid case, consists of a pendulum that swings on a pivot, and is set in motion like a hand-wound clock.

386
00:29:50,401 --> 00:29:57,400
Below the pivot, there's a fixed weight. Above it, a sliding one.

387
00:29:57,401 --> 00:30:02,400
When the sliding weight is moved to a given point on the pendulum,

388
00:30:02,401 --> 00:30:07,400
it changes the speed at which the pendulum swings.

389
00:30:07,401 --> 00:30:10,400
A scale of numbers indicates the beats per minute.

390
00:30:10,401 --> 00:30:16,400
And the click? An escapement makes the sound as the wheel passes a pallet.

391
00:30:16,401 --> 00:30:18,231
It was simply a wind-up metronome.

392
00:30:18,232 --> 00:30:24,640
You turned it on and it would go click, click, click, and you've tried to follow that click as accurately as you could.

393
00:30:25,200 --> 00:30:28,760
And then, of course, technology got involved and improved it.

394
00:30:29,920 --> 00:30:40,880
Modern companies such as Franz have replaced the classic pendulum device with quartz precision electronic blips, flashing lights, and even a sampled human voice.

395
00:30:40,881 --> 00:30:48,900
The original concept of the metronome that helped composers and musicians of the past keep perfect time

396
00:30:48,901 --> 00:30:54,520
still resonates in musical gadgets that shake, rattle, and roll.

397
00:30:56,560 --> 00:30:58,160
Simply press click.

398
00:30:59,020 --> 00:31:01,620
It allows you to change the interval.

399
00:31:03,600 --> 00:31:07,260
The drum machine has added some new riffs to the metronome story.

400
00:31:08,260 --> 00:31:09,980
It still drives the beat.

401
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:14,360
But this contraption has a computer brain that creates the music.

402
00:31:16,700 --> 00:31:19,560
Gadgets with rhythm are part of a revolution.

403
00:31:21,220 --> 00:31:25,760
Fifty years ago, they began changing the way music is composed and played.

404
00:31:27,840 --> 00:31:30,460
Well, this gadget is an old Wurlitzer drum machine.

405
00:31:30,600 --> 00:31:36,520
It's one of the very first drum machines, and it was used for the home organ market,

406
00:31:36,960 --> 00:31:39,560
so you could have a rhythm section in your home.

407
00:31:39,561 --> 00:31:43,720
The earliest model Wurlitzer was called a Sideman.

408
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:47,060
Like a more evolved and musical metronome,

409
00:31:47,460 --> 00:31:51,220
it contained a large rotating wheel that forced small metal brushes

410
00:31:51,221 --> 00:31:54,320
to generate a limited repertoire of mechanical sounds.

411
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:57,920
Here's your tango.

412
00:32:00,200 --> 00:32:02,660
And the kids went crazy for the cha-cha.

413
00:32:02,661 --> 00:32:09,940
Next came a pre-digital technology called synthesis.

414
00:32:10,940 --> 00:32:13,740
An old drum machine was an old synthesizer,

415
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:16,220
and it's usually taking sound like white noise,

416
00:32:16,360 --> 00:32:18,580
which is all the frequency, like a shh sound,

417
00:32:18,900 --> 00:32:22,240
and using filtering to make it sound like a snare drum.

418
00:32:23,400 --> 00:32:24,940
It didn't sound real.

419
00:32:25,380 --> 00:32:26,600
It didn't sound like music.

420
00:32:27,360 --> 00:32:30,300
It was loud, electronic, and pre-programmed.

421
00:32:30,301 --> 00:32:35,400
Musicians wanted an interactive gadget that sounded authentic.

422
00:32:36,340 --> 00:32:41,060
By the 80s, a computer technology called sampling delivered the real thing.

423
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:45,200
Sampling is basically a recording technology.

424
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:49,200
Instead of recording into a tape recorder, we're recording into a computer

425
00:32:49,201 --> 00:32:54,700
and converting it from an analog source of a microphone going in into a digital source.

426
00:32:55,220 --> 00:32:58,940
And now I play it back on my keyboard, it sounds just like that cymbal hit

427
00:32:58,941 --> 00:33:02,700
or the snare and the kick. So that's how I build my drum machine sound.

428
00:33:07,980 --> 00:33:10,900
Originally, everyone wanted the drum machine to sound real.

429
00:33:12,040 --> 00:33:14,060
But what goes around comes around.

430
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:18,420
And eventually, the synth sound itself caught on.

431
00:33:19,180 --> 00:33:21,000
Especially with disco and rap.

432
00:33:21,820 --> 00:33:24,620
This next gadget is called the LIN-9000.

433
00:33:25,080 --> 00:33:27,620
This is very sophisticated in terms of programming.

434
00:33:27,621 --> 00:33:29,820
I mean, you can actually put in your own sounds.

435
00:33:30,680 --> 00:33:31,740
Sounds like this.

436
00:33:34,160 --> 00:33:36,960
And like all of them, you can play on top of it.

437
00:33:43,520 --> 00:33:45,940
So even when you buy a modern drum machine now,

438
00:33:46,120 --> 00:33:48,000
it has very realistic drum sounds,

439
00:33:48,001 --> 00:33:51,600
but it also has a good handful of those electronic sounds that are classic.

440
00:33:51,601 --> 00:33:58,600
The drum gadget for the digital generation is called the groove box.

441
00:33:58,601 --> 00:34:06,600
With a wave of the hand, a musician, or non-musician, becomes both composer and one-man band.

442
00:34:06,601 --> 00:34:13,600
In other words, even if you can't write a note of music or play a single instrument,

443
00:34:13,601 --> 00:34:16,600
this gadget allows you to jam with the band.

444
00:34:16,601 --> 00:34:20,560
band

445
00:34:20,561 --> 00:34:22,600
so that's the drum machines

446
00:34:22,601 --> 00:34:28,440
i am in the hand of the human drum scene that is can replace me

447
00:34:28,441 --> 00:34:33,360
this tube radio probably thought the same thing

448
00:34:33,361 --> 00:34:40,360
the transistor creates the quintessential post-war gadget

449
00:34:40,361 --> 00:34:43,220
It barely caught the eye of a war-weary public.

450
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:49,280
But in 1947, something big happened in a telecommunications lab.

451
00:34:49,860 --> 00:34:52,900
And it came in the form of the tiny transistor.

452
00:34:55,940 --> 00:34:59,560
The transistor marked a milestone in the information age.

453
00:35:01,240 --> 00:35:06,140
Changing the guts of the radio, television, and computer.

454
00:35:07,060 --> 00:35:09,340
And inspiring a new generation of gadgets.

455
00:35:09,341 --> 00:35:12,340
The transistor is the future.

456
00:35:14,340 --> 00:35:17,340
Prior to transistor times, we had to use vacuum tubes.

457
00:35:17,341 --> 00:35:19,340
Vacuum tubes are, number one, bulky.

458
00:35:19,341 --> 00:35:21,340
Number two, they're up to break.

459
00:35:21,341 --> 00:35:23,340
They take a lot of power.

460
00:35:23,341 --> 00:35:29,340
Whereas the transistor is a device which is compact, light,

461
00:35:29,341 --> 00:35:33,340
takes very little power, turns on instantly.

462
00:35:33,341 --> 00:35:37,340
In 1947, AT&T assigned a group of scientists,

463
00:35:37,341 --> 00:35:43,660
scientists, headed by William Shockley, to explore the possibility of replacing the vacuum

464
00:35:43,661 --> 00:35:52,100
tubes used to amplify telephone signals with a smaller, more reliable device.

465
00:35:52,101 --> 00:35:53,496
Even the name was new.

466
00:35:53,497 --> 00:35:58,380
It combined the transistor's dual function, the trans coming from transmitter

467
00:35:58,381 --> 00:36:03,300
or transfer, and the sister coming from resistor.

468
00:36:03,301 --> 00:36:10,800
In other words, it's a resistor to which you can transfer information by putting a voltage on it.

469
00:36:10,801 --> 00:36:17,200
Well, to make a long story short, they tried doing this with a common semiconductor at the time called germanium.

470
00:36:17,201 --> 00:36:24,700
Germanium, like silicon, is a solid material that sometimes conducts, sometimes insulates.

471
00:36:24,701 --> 00:36:29,700
Small amounts of electrical current regulate the flow of electrons,

472
00:36:29,701 --> 00:36:33,460
thus allowing for rapid changes in electrical signal.

473
00:36:35,360 --> 00:36:38,340
In addition, the signal emerges as strong as when it entered.

474
00:36:40,580 --> 00:36:43,920
The result was a wonder of electrical engineering,

475
00:36:44,620 --> 00:36:47,920
a conveniently tiny amplifier that never burned out.

476
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:53,840
It didn't take the radio industry long to see the potential of this tiny dynamo.

477
00:36:53,841 --> 00:37:00,780
The transistor radio was the first gadget powered by transistors.

478
00:37:01,620 --> 00:37:04,720
It debuted in 1954 with the Regency,

479
00:37:05,300 --> 00:37:10,140
a stunningly small radio that would kick off the revolution of high-tech miniaturization.

480
00:37:13,460 --> 00:37:15,580
Transistors were rare, hard to find.

481
00:37:15,680 --> 00:37:16,480
They were very expensive.

482
00:37:16,740 --> 00:37:19,420
You could probably buy one for maybe $5 if you were lucky,

483
00:37:19,660 --> 00:37:21,660
and that was a lot of money back in the 50s.

484
00:37:21,661 --> 00:37:27,180
And I remember having lunch with Shockley, and he was telling us at the table, he says,

485
00:37:27,240 --> 00:37:30,860
you know, he says, those guys from RCA came to visit today.

486
00:37:31,040 --> 00:37:32,640
And he says, you know what they're talking about?

487
00:37:33,100 --> 00:37:38,640
He says, they're talking about making transistors and selling them for 50 cents a piece.

488
00:37:39,320 --> 00:37:41,540
And we all looked at them in horror.

489
00:37:41,780 --> 00:37:43,920
I mean, everyone thought, of course, they were really crazy.

490
00:37:45,020 --> 00:37:50,060
The Bobby Soxers were the first to take notice and turned up the volume on change.

491
00:37:50,061 --> 00:37:53,900
It just evoked so many things of the era.

492
00:37:54,120 --> 00:37:58,720
Rock and roll music, going to the beach, baseball.

493
00:37:59,100 --> 00:38:02,220
It was portable. It was modern. It was sexy.

494
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:04,100
It was cutting-edge technology.

495
00:38:05,180 --> 00:38:08,260
Famous brand names were there to ride the radio wave.

496
00:38:09,320 --> 00:38:10,540
The very first RCA.

497
00:38:11,600 --> 00:38:12,820
The first Motorola.

498
00:38:15,040 --> 00:38:18,100
Raytheon produced its first model with removable transistors,

499
00:38:18,101 --> 00:38:21,580
still fearing the burnout of the old vacuum tube.

500
00:38:24,380 --> 00:38:29,600
In 1957, a Japanese company, Tokyo Sushen Kogo Limited,

501
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:32,420
was more confident about the transistor's future

502
00:38:32,421 --> 00:38:35,860
and hardwired the transistors into the radio.

503
00:38:38,160 --> 00:38:42,760
The TR-63 became the company's first export to the United States.

504
00:38:44,160 --> 00:38:46,720
The radio became so popular in America

505
00:38:46,721 --> 00:38:50,160
that the company changed its name to Sony.

506
00:38:52,720 --> 00:38:56,920
The Sony 610 sold half a million in 1959,

507
00:38:58,120 --> 00:39:01,100
foreshadowing the company's dominance in consumer electronics.

508
00:39:03,540 --> 00:39:06,400
Designers mimicked pop culture and auto styling

509
00:39:06,401 --> 00:39:10,520
with speedometer-like tuning dials and images of speed.

510
00:39:11,640 --> 00:39:16,000
The Motorola Jetwing evokes the excitement of the supersonic age.

511
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:23,720
The Emerson 888 series designed its radios after the pre-NASA space programs of the military.

512
00:39:23,721 --> 00:39:28,720
The space race metaphor was more than fitting for these radios,

513
00:39:28,721 --> 00:39:35,720
since transistors made possible the compact communication and navigation devices on manned spacecraft.

514
00:39:35,721 --> 00:39:42,720
and navigation devices on manned spacecraft.

515
00:39:42,721 --> 00:39:47,720
The transistor radio was the adolescent soul of the 1950s and 60s.

516
00:39:47,721 --> 00:39:53,720
Like many classic gadgets, it had the perfect convergence of technology and design.

517
00:39:53,721 --> 00:39:56,720
The tiny transistor made it portable,

518
00:39:56,721 --> 00:40:03,720
but bold, even brilliant design made it a coveted object of desire.

519
00:40:03,721 --> 00:40:04,560
Gadgets aren't born.

520
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:06,520
They're designed.

521
00:40:07,580 --> 00:40:11,940
An industrial designer is the person that brings the wow factor,

522
00:40:12,380 --> 00:40:14,720
the person that makes you fall in love with a product.

523
00:40:15,320 --> 00:40:19,120
Our role is to bring magic into inanimate objects.

524
00:40:21,200 --> 00:40:26,340
Herbst Lazar Bell specializes in the art of turning good gadgets into good-looking gadgets.

525
00:40:27,340 --> 00:40:31,660
They put their stamp on everything from an NFL coach's wireless headset

526
00:40:31,661 --> 00:40:35,260
to an interactive gas pump.

527
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:43,880
For 30 years, HLB has turned exotic, even improbable products into classics.

528
00:40:44,760 --> 00:40:48,080
The legendary Ron Popeil was their first client.

529
00:40:48,460 --> 00:40:53,900
So we were the guys that actually created things like pocket fisherman and kitchen magician

530
00:40:53,901 --> 00:40:59,700
and steam set and trim comb and, oh God, it just goes on and on.

531
00:40:59,701 --> 00:41:00,820
Those were true gadgets.

532
00:41:01,660 --> 00:41:06,320
Gadgets that have been embraced by the public all share one thing in common.

533
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:08,580
Or is it two?

534
00:41:09,540 --> 00:41:13,780
There really needs to be a balance of form and function.

535
00:41:14,580 --> 00:41:17,920
And whereas I think a lot of gadgets or products that are considered gadgets

536
00:41:17,921 --> 00:41:20,600
are driven more by the function and technology,

537
00:41:21,460 --> 00:41:26,820
I think there are also many, great many products that are driven almost exclusively by the form.

538
00:41:26,821 --> 00:41:29,820
You know, it would probably make a lot of sense if it was square.

539
00:41:29,821 --> 00:41:34,680
designers and engineers must work as a team to strike this delicate balance

540
00:41:34,681 --> 00:41:42,540
bringing a product from vision to physical reality is the ultimate goal for herbst lazar bell

541
00:41:42,541 --> 00:41:50,700
but to design a gadget with a soul the creative team first must allow themselves full freedom to

542
00:41:50,701 --> 00:41:57,780
dream i don't want to hear any judgment on anything that anybody says today absolutely

543
00:41:57,781 --> 00:42:02,120
nothing. What you want to do is you want to start out with this great big funnel

544
00:42:02,121 --> 00:42:06,080
of great ideas and we'll just keep bringing them down until it makes some

545
00:42:06,081 --> 00:42:10,540
sense. We need to get really, really, really dopey. Okay, you want to come up?

546
00:42:10,541 --> 00:42:15,020
Absolutely. And so the design team sails off into uncharted waters.

547
00:42:15,960 --> 00:42:18,920
What would you do? Just play with me a little bit.

548
00:42:20,260 --> 00:42:24,040
A gadget currently on the drawing board scans a garment's barcode

549
00:42:24,041 --> 00:42:27,980
and actually projects a hologram of the user wearing the outfit.

550
00:42:31,320 --> 00:42:34,020
Call them crazy, call them unrealistic.

551
00:42:34,780 --> 00:42:37,260
But don't call them if you want a boring-looking gadget.

552
00:42:38,660 --> 00:42:40,260
It's called Zuzu's Petals.

553
00:42:40,940 --> 00:42:46,600
It's a personal digital assistant in the form of a house plant,

554
00:42:46,780 --> 00:42:51,040
which sits on the window and absorbs light from the sun's energy.

555
00:42:51,041 --> 00:42:55,360
It goes into digital dirt, which is down below its memory and power.

556
00:42:55,800 --> 00:42:59,020
And it has these leaves, these petals, which can be worn like jewelry.

557
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:05,580
The digital image capture or the digital audio capture get returned at the end of the day to Zuzu.

558
00:43:06,060 --> 00:43:08,480
She downloads. She stores the information.

559
00:43:09,040 --> 00:43:12,320
The PDA, which fans out like two petals from a flower,

560
00:43:12,520 --> 00:43:15,980
weaves a holograph that gives you your desktop or your website.

561
00:43:15,981 --> 00:43:21,360
And when you close it up, you could wear this over your neck as a pennant, that kind of a thing.

562
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:26,440
So it's a friendly, much more approachable, much more aesthetic solution to what a computer could be,

563
00:43:26,720 --> 00:43:29,620
instead of that plain old beige or black box.

564
00:43:30,340 --> 00:43:34,180
It's impossible to predict the impact of a wild idea like Zuzu.

565
00:43:35,180 --> 00:43:39,700
A gadget that transcends gadgetdom is rare and wonderful.

566
00:43:40,220 --> 00:43:45,520
It must at once seize on new technologies and embrace bold new designs.

567
00:43:45,980 --> 00:43:50,300
The final product must be something we can't imagine living without.

568
00:43:51,300 --> 00:43:53,760
From the flower pot that led to the flashlight,

569
00:43:54,380 --> 00:43:57,420
to the microprocessor that wants to be a flower,

570
00:43:58,220 --> 00:44:02,020
gadgets continue to invent and reinvent themselves.

571
00:44:02,700 --> 00:44:05,820
I am very excited about all the changes that are happening.

572
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:08,120
It's like a kid in a candy store.

573
00:44:08,121 --> 00:44:14,560
You look around and the rate of innovation is so fast

574
00:44:14,561 --> 00:44:19,960
that it's if you wish for something, you read about it the next day.

575
00:44:20,660 --> 00:44:22,940
It is tremendously empowering.

